<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#bard">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bard</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as bardd, bàrd</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A bard was a poet and singer who composed and performed verse in the service of a patron, a calling attested among the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celts">Celts</a> and continued in the medieval Celtic-speaking world. Greek and Roman authors writing about <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> between the 1st century BCE and the 4th century CE count the bards, with the druids and the vates, among its honoured learned orders, singing the praises of chieftains and the satire of their rivals to an instrument like the lyre. In medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> bardd remained the usual word for a poet, while Ireland kept bard for a grade below the file; in both, praise and satire of rulers stayed central. The word now serves loosely for any poet.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bard.jpg" alt="Bard" width="330" height="469" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Benjamin West · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Benjamin_West_-_The_Bard_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word goes back to the Proto-Celtic noun *bardos, a poet-singer, itself derived from a Proto-Indo-European compound meaning praise-maker. It is attested in Gaulish as bardus in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and bárdoi in Greek, and survives in Middle Welsh bardd, Middle Irish bard and Middle Breton barz. The fullest early accounts descend from the lost history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/posidonius">Posidonius</a>, who travelled in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> in the early 1st century BCE, and reach us through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus">Diodorus Siculus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> and Athenaeus. Because these writers share one source they count as a single tradition. Caesar, who wrote at length about the druids, does not mention the bards at all.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The sources agree on what the bard did: praise poetry and satire, sung to a stringed instrument that Diodorus compares to a lyre. The power to raise or lower a patron's standing gave successful bards a privileged and well-paid position. Athenaeus, citing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/posidonius">Posidonius</a>, tells of the Arvernian Louernios, to whom a poet arrived late after a feast and sang of the king's greatness; Louernios threw him a bag of gold, and the poet answered with a second song. Appian describes a singer in the train of an embassy sent by the Arvernian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bituitus">Bituitus</a>, who praised the king, his people and the envoy. Diodorus adds that bards, together with druids, could step between two armies and halt them.</p><p>In medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> the court poet held an office set out in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyfraith-hywel">Laws of Hywel Dda</a>, with the pencerdd, or chief of song, at its head. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poets-of-the-princes">Gogynfeirdd</a> of the 12th and 13th centuries composed praise and elegy for the princes of independent Wales, and the order broke up in the late 16th and early 17th centuries. In Ireland the professional poet was the file, and the baird were held to rely on talent rather than formal learning. Irish bardic poetry lasted into the 17th century, and in Gaelic Scotland the MacMhuirich family kept the classical tradition into the early 18th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>A Welsh tradition holds that Edward I had the Welsh bards put to death after the conquest of 1282, but no medieval source records it, and the historian Prys Morgan treats it as a fable. Thomas Gray's ode The Bard, published in 1757, fixed the legend in the public mind. James Macpherson's Works of Ossian, published in the 1760s as the verse of an ancient Highland bard, was read across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, Edward Williams, writing as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iolo-morganwg">Iolo Morganwg</a>, founded the Gorsedd Beirdd Ynys Prydein in 1792, supporting his claims with forgeries exposed only after his death. The image of an aged, visionary bard that grew from these works remains familiar in popular culture.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bard” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
